==Trivia==
*In the comics, Cassandra Nova is the twin sister of [[Charles Xavier]] and enemy of the [[X-Men]], who is most famous for orchestrating the genocide on Genosha, killing more than fifteen million mutant residents with a rogue [[Sentinels|Sentinel]].
*In ''[[w:c:xmenmovies:X-Men:_The_Last_Stand|X-Men: The Last Stand]]'', Charles was revealed to have a brain-dead twin ''brother'' named [[w:c:xmenmovies:P. Xavier|P. Xavier]], whose body he inhabited to escape death at the hands of the [[w:c:xmenmovies:Jean Grey|Dark Phoenix]]. This would make him a male variant of Cassandra Nova, much like how [[Sylvie Laufeydottir]] is a female variant of [[Loki]].
*She is the fifth female main antagonist in a [[Marvel Cinematic Universe]] film, following [[Hela]], [[Ghost]], [[Scarlet Witch]] and [[Dar-Benn]].

==Behind the Scenes== 
*[[Emma Corrin]]'s casting as Cassandra Nova was because ''[[Deadpool & Wolverine]]'' director [[Shawn Levy]] saw them in a performance of {{WPS|Virginia Woolf}}'s ''{{WPS|Orlando: A Biography}}'' stage play in 2022, with Corrin accepting the role as they were interested in playing a villain.<ref>[https://www.empireonline.com/movies/news/emma-corrin-villain-deadpool-3/ Emma Corrin On The OA Creators’ New Show A Murder At The End Of The World: ‘It’s So Ahead Of Its Time’]</ref>
*[[Marvel Studios]] eyed Emma Corrin to play Cassandra Nova since late 2022 around {{WPS|Christmas}}, but had to work out their busy schedule to make sure they could fully commit to the role, settling any arrangements around early February 2023.<ref>[https://deadline.com/2023/02/deadpool-3-emma-corrin-lands-lead-role-in-marvel-studios-sequel-1235258306/ ‘Deadpool 3’: Emma Corrin Lands Lead Role In Marvel Studios Sequel]</ref>
*Emma Corrin was praised by [[Ryan Reynolds (actor)|Ryan Reynolds]] and [[Hugh Jackman]] for being like "a skin-covered {{WPS|Swiss Army knife}}" to work with and their ability to "subtly change-to turn on a dime" with an effortlessness while portraying Cassandra Nova, with Reynolds feeling that Corrin brought a {{WPS|Gene Wilder}}-type of energy to Nova, made her motivations easier to understand and humanized her dialogue by inspiring themself on {{WPS|Christopher Waltz}}'s {{WPS|Hans Landa}} from ''{{WPS|Inglourious Basterds}}''.<ref>[https://www.harpersbazaar.com/culture/film-tv/a60814103/emma-corrin-interview-june-july-2024/ Emma Corrin Is in a League of Their Own]</ref>

